content:
CS409 MP2 — Learn React, TypeScript, JavaScript, HTML, CSS, and API
Programming from Zero
I'll teach you the languages and technologies you need to understand and build your Pokémon
Explorer MP2, with explanations, examples, and reusable template code.
Since you already have programming experience, I'll focus on what is different about web
programming rather than spending too much time on concepts like loops and functions.
By the end, you should understand how to write a React application yourself, request data from
an API, build interactive pages, and deploy the result.
1. The technology stack
Your project uses several technologies, but you don't need to learn six completely different
programming languages.
Technology         What it does                                      Importance
HTML               Defines webpage structure                         Essential basics
CSS                Controls appearance and layout                    Essential basics
JavaScript         Adds logic and interactivity                      Very important
TypeScript         Adds static types to JavaScript                   Very important
React              Builds interactive user interfaces                Most important
Axios              Sends HTTP requests to APIs                       Important
React Router       Controls navigation between pages                 Important
Vite               Runs and builds your project                      Basic understanding
Technically, TypeScript is a programming language, React is a library, and Vite is a build tool.
Here's the relationship:

                                    Your Pokémon Website

                                     What the user sees in the browser

               React                           TypeScript                          CSS
         UI & interactions                    Logic & types                     Appearance

                                           Axios + PokéAPI
                                          Requests and JSON data
       Vite provides the development server and production build tooling around this application.

2. JavaScript fundamentals
JavaScript is the foundation of React and TypeScript. You need to be comfortable reading
JavaScript before moving into React.
2.1 Variables: let and const
const name = "Pikachu";
let level = 25;
let isFavorite = true;


level = 26;                  // Allowed
// name = "Eevee";           // Error


    const  : the variable cannot be reassigned.
    let : the variable can be reassigned.
    var : older style; generally avoid it in new code.

Important nuance: const does not make objects immutable.
const pokemon = { name: "Pikachu", level: 25 };


pokemon.level = 26; // Allowed

2.2 Arrays and objects
An array stores a collection of values.
const names = ["Pikachu", "Eevee", "Bulbasaur"];


console.log(names[0]);        // Pikachu
console.log(names.length); // 3


An object stores named properties.
const pokemon = {
     id: 25,
     name: "Pikachu",
     type: "Electric",
     weight: 6
};


console.log(pokemon.name);            // Pikachu
console.log(pokemon["type"]); // Electric


Objects are especially important because API responses are commonly represented as JSON
objects.
2.3 Functions and arrow functions
Traditional function:
function double(x) {
     return x * 2;
}


Arrow function:
const double = (x) => {
     return x * 2;
};

Short form:
const double = x => x * 2;


These examples compute the same result, although arrow functions and ordinary functions
differ in how they handle this and some other language features.
You'll see arrow functions everywhere in React.
2.4 The four most important array methods
These four methods are extremely useful in MP2.
A. map() — transform every item
const nums = [1, 2, 3];
const doubled = nums.map(x => x * 2);


// [2, 4, 6]


map()  creates a new array by applying a function to each item.
B. filter() — keep matching items
const nums = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5];
const large = nums.filter(x => x > 3);


// [4, 5]


In MP2, you could use this to retain Pokémon matching a search or type filter.
C. find() — return the first match
const nums = [10, 20, 30];
const result = nums.find(x => x > 15);


// 20


If nothing matches, it returns undefined .

D. sort() — order items
const nums = [10, 2, 30];


nums.sort((a, b) => a - b);
// [2, 10, 30]


The comparator returns a negative number when a should come before b , a positive number
when b should come first, and zero when they are equivalent for sorting.
Warning: .sort() mutates its array, so use a copy when sorting React state:
const sorted = [...nums].sort((a, b) => a - b);



2.5 Destructuring
Destructuring extracts values from objects or arrays.
const pokemon = {
     id: 25,
     name: "Pikachu",
     weight: 6
};


const { name, weight } = pokemon;


console.log(name);        // Pikachu
console.log(weight); // 6


Array destructuring:
const coordinates = [10, 20];
const [x, y] = coordinates;


You'll use this exact syntax in React:
const [count, setCount] = useState(0);

The first variable holds the current state value. The second is a function for updating that value.
2.6 Spread syntax
The ... syntax copies or combines array elements or object properties.
const original = [1, 2, 3];
const copy = [...original];


const pokemon = { name: "Pikachu", level: 5 };


const updated = {
     ...pokemon,
     level: 10
};


This matters because React state should generally be updated without mutating the existing
object or array.
2.7 Template strings
Template strings use backticks and ${...} .
const name = "Pikachu";
const id = 25;


const message = `Pokemon ${name} has ID ${id}`;


They are useful for API endpoints and dynamic text.
const url = `https://pokeapi.co/api/v2/pokemon/${id}`;



3. TypeScript fundamentals
TypeScript is JavaScript with a static type system. It helps catch mistakes before the browser
runs your code.

3.1 Basic types
let name: string = "Pikachu";
let level: number = 25;
let isLegendary: boolean = false;


let types: string[] = ["Electric"];
let stats: number[] = [35, 55, 40];


TypeScript checks assignments:
let level: number = 25;


// level = "twenty-five";        // Type error



3.2 TypeScript interfaces
Interfaces describe the expected structure of an object.
interface Pokemon {
     id: number;
     name: string;
     weight: number;
     types: string[];
}


Now you can write:
const pikachu: Pokemon = {
     id: 25,
     name: "Pikachu",
     weight: 60,
     types: ["electric"]
};


If you forget a required property or provide the wrong type, TypeScript reports an error.

Be careful about units: PokéAPI reports weight in hectograms and height in decimeters. A value
of 60 for Pikachu means 6 kilograms, not 60 kilograms.
3.3 Optional properties
Sometimes an API field may be missing.
interface Pokemon {
    id: number;
    name: string;
    image?: string;
}


The ? means image is optional.
You can safely access it using a fallback:
const imageUrl = pokemon.image ?? "/placeholder.png";


??   uses the right-hand value when the left-hand value is null or undefined .
3.4 Union types
A union allows several specific values.
type SortDirection = "asc" | "desc";


let direction: SortDirection = "asc";


This prevents invalid values such as "upwards" .
3.5 Function types
function formatName(name: string): string {
    return name.toUpperCase();
}


function getNextId(id: number): number {

    return id + 1;
}


The type after the parameter name describes the input; the type after the closing parenthesis
describes the return value.
3.6 Why .tsx files exist
     .ts : TypeScript without JSX.
     .tsx : TypeScript containing JSX syntax, commonly used for React components.

For example, a React component returning <h1>Hello</h1> belongs in a .tsx file.
4. HTML and JSX
HTML defines the structure of a webpage.
<h1>Pokémon Explorer</h1>
<p>Explore your favorite Pokémon.</p>
<button>Search</button>


React uses JSX, which resembles HTML but is written inside JavaScript or TypeScript.
function Welcome() {
    const name = "Pikachu";


    return (
         <div>
           <h1>Hello, {name}!</h1>
           <p>Welcome to Pokémon Explorer.</p>
         </div>
    );
}


Important JSX rules:
    Use className instead of HTML's class .
    Put JavaScript expressions inside {} .
    Return one root element, or use a fragment <>...</> .

   Close all JSX tags.
   Use onClick rather than HTML's lowercase onclick .
Example:
function Greeting() {
    return (
         <section className="greeting">
           <h2>Hello!</h2>
           <button onClick={() => alert("Clicked!")}>
            Click me
           </button>
         </section>
    );
}



5. CSS essentials
CSS controls how HTML elements appear.
body {
    font-family: Arial, sans-serif;
    background-color: #f5f5f5;
    margin: 0;
}


h1 {
    color: #2563eb;
}


.card {
    padding: 16px;
    border: 1px solid #ddd;
    border-radius: 12px;
}


A CSS class begins with a dot when written in the stylesheet:

.pokemon-card {
    background: white;
}


In JSX, you apply it through className :
<div className="pokemon-card">
    Pikachu
</div>



Flexbox
Flexbox is good for navigation bars and horizontal layouts.
.navbar {
    display: flex;
    justify-content: space-between;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 16px;
}



CSS Grid
Grid is especially useful for a Pokémon gallery.
.gallery {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(3, 1fr);
    gap: 20px;
}


For responsive layouts:
.gallery {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns:
      repeat(auto-fit, minmax(180px, 1fr));

    gap: 20px;
}


This lets the browser automatically adjust the number of columns depending on available width.
6. React fundamentals — The most important part
React lets you describe how an interface should look based on its current data.
Instead of manually changing the HTML every time something happens, you update state, and
React renders the updated interface.
6.1 Your first component
function PokemonCard() {
    return (
         <div>
           <h2>Pikachu</h2>
           <p>Type: Electric</p>
         </div>
    );
}


export default PokemonCard;


What each part means:
         function PokemonCard()    defines the component.
         return (...) describes the interface.
         <div> , <h2> , and <p> are JSX elements.
         export default allows the component to be imported by another file.


6.2 Props — Pass data into components
Hardcoding Pikachu inside every card wouldn't be useful.
Instead, use props.
interface PokemonCardProps {
    name: string;

    type: string;
}


function PokemonCard({ name, type }: PokemonCardProps) {
    return (
         <div>
           <h2>{name}</h2>
           <p>Type: {type}</p>
         </div>
    );
}


Now a parent component can reuse it:
<PokemonCard name="Pikachu" type="Electric" />
<PokemonCard name="Charmander" type="Fire" />


This is conceptually similar to calling a function with different arguments.
6.3 useState — Make the interface interactive
A regular local variable does not preserve component state across React renders.
useState solves this.


import { useState } from "react";


function Counter() {
    const [count, setCount] = useState(0);


    return (
         <div>
           <p>Count: {count}</p>


           <button onClick={() => setCount(c => c + 1)}>
            Increase
           </button>
         </div>
    );
}

export default Counter;


Try the same behavior here:
                                               0
                              Decrease           Increase Reset
                          Changing state produces a new rendered value.
Here's what happens when you click Increase:
  1. The event handler calls setCount .
  2. React schedules an update.
  3. React calls the component again with its updated state.
  4. The interface displays the new count.
Notice that state setters do not immediately change the value captured by the currently
executing function.
6.4 Controlled inputs
A search field should update React state when the user types.
import { useState } from "react";


function SearchExample() {
  const [search, setSearch] = useState("");


  return (
    <div>
       <input
         value={search}
         onChange={e => setSearch(e.target.value)}
         placeholder="Search..."
       />


       <p>You typed: {search}</p>
    </div>

    );
}


There are two important connections:
 value={search} makes the input display the current state.

 onChange={...} updates that state when the user types.

Together, these form a controlled input.
6.5 Conditional rendering
Sometimes you only want to show an element when a condition is true.
function Status({ loading }: { loading: boolean }) {
    if (loading) {
         return <p>Loading...</p>;
    }


    return <p>Data loaded successfully!</p>;
}


Another form:
{error && <p>Something went wrong.</p>}


This is useful for API error messages, loading indicators, and empty search results.
6.6 Rendering an array
React commonly uses .map() to convert data into UI elements.
const names = ["Pikachu", "Eevee", "Charmander"];


function PokemonList() {
    return (
         <ul>
           {names.map(name => (

            <li key={name}>{name}</li>
           ))}
         </ul>
    );
}


The key helps React identify each item between renders. Prefer a stable unique Pokémon ID
when available.
7. Asynchronous programming — async , await , and Promises
This is one of the most important topics for API programming.
Imagine requesting Pokémon data from a remote server. The response is not immediately
available.
JavaScript handles this with asynchronous operations.
7.1 Promise
A Promise represents a result that may become available later.
const promise = fetch("https://pokeapi.co/api/v2/pokemon/25");


The request returns a Promise rather than the Pokémon JSON directly.
7.2 async and await
async function loadPokemon() {
    const response = await fetch(
         "https://pokeapi.co/api/v2/pokemon/25"
    );


    if (!response.ok) {
         throw new Error("HTTP request failed");
    }


    const data = await response.json();

    console.log(data.name);
}


        async  declares an asynchronous function.
        await pauses that function until the Promise settles.
        Other JavaScript tasks can continue while the operation is pending.
        response.json() also returns a Promise.


7.3 try , catch , and finally
Network requests can fail.
async function loadPokemon() {
    try {
        const response = await fetch(
             "https://pokeapi.co/api/v2/pokemon/25"
        );


        if (!response.ok) {
             throw new Error(`HTTP error: ${response.status}`);
        }


        const data = await response.json();
        console.log(data.name);
    } catch (error) {
        console.error("Failed to load Pokémon:", error);
    } finally {
        console.log("Request finished");
    }
}


finally   executes whether the request succeeds or fails.
For MP2, you will typically connect this to loading and error state.
8. Axios — Communicating with an API
Axios is an HTTP client library that simplifies network requests.
Install it in an existing npm project:

npm install axios



8.1 Basic GET request
import axios from "axios";


async function getPokemon(id: number) {
    const response = await axios.get(
         `https://pokeapi.co/api/v2/pokemon/${id}`
    );


    return response.data;
}


Unlike native fetch , Axios places parsed response data in response.data .
8.2 Typed API response
A useful reusable template:
import axios from "axios";


interface ExampleData {
    id: number;
    name: string;
}


async function getData(id: number): Promise<ExampleData> {
    const response = await axios.get<ExampleData>(
         `https://example.com/api/items/${id}`
    );


    return response.data;
}


The <ExampleData> describes the expected response type. It helps TypeScript check your
code, but it does not validate the response at runtime. Promise based…

8.3 Promise.all()
Suppose you want several records.
async function getMultiple(ids: number[]) {
    const requests = ids.map(id =>
         axios.get(`https://pokeapi.co/api/v2/pokemon/${id}`)
    );


    const responses = await Promise.all(requests);


    return responses.map(response => response.data);
}


Instead of waiting for each request sequentially, Promise.all() allows the requests to
proceed concurrently.
Important: a single rejected request causes Promise.all() to reject. For larger datasets, use
appropriate batching, concurrency limits, or partial-failure handling.
9. useEffect — Connect React to external data
useEffect    is commonly used to synchronize a React component with external systems,
including network requests.
Here is a generic example you can adapt to your own API.
import { useEffect, useState } from "react";
import axios from "axios";


interface Item {
    id: number;
    title: string;
}


function ExamplePage() {
    const [item, setItem] = useState<Item | null>(null);
    const [loading, setLoading] = useState(true);
    const [error, setError] = useState("");

    useEffect(() => {
      let active = true;


      async function loadItem() {
           try {
               const response = await axios.get<Item>(
                    "https://jsonplaceholder.typicode.com/todos/1"
               );


               if (active) {
                    setItem(response.data);
               }
           } catch {
               if (active) {
                    setError("Unable to load the item.");
               }
           } finally {
               if (active) {
                    setLoading(false);
               }
           }
      }


      loadItem();


      return () => {
           active = false;
      };
    }, []);


    if (loading) return <p>Loading...</p>;
    if (error) return <p>{error}</p>;


    return <h1>{item?.title}</h1>;
}


The example uses a public placeholder endpoint, not the MP2 solution.
The critical syntax is:

useEffect(() => {
    // Synchronize with an external system.
}, []);


The empty dependency array means the effect is not rerun because of changing component
state or props. In development, React Strict Mode may still run an extra setup/cleanup cycle to
help reveal bugs.
If the effect depends on a changing ID, that ID belongs in the dependency array.
The active cleanup guard prevents an outdated request from updating state after cleanup,
though it does not cancel the network request itself.
10. React Router — Multiple pages
React Router lets the browser URL determine which component is displayed.
A generic example using the declarative React Router API:
import {
    BrowserRouter,
    Routes,
    Route,
    Link
} from "react-router";


function Home() {
    return <h1>Home page</h1>;
}


function About() {
    return <h1>About page</h1>;
}


function App() {
    return (
      <BrowserRouter>
           <nav>
            <Link to="/">Home</Link>
            <Link to="/about">About</Link>

           </nav>


           <Routes>
            <Route path="/" element={<Home />} />
            <Route path="/about" element={<About />} />
           </Routes>
         </BrowserRouter>
    );
}


export default App;


In older React Router versions you may see imports from react-router-dom . Use the imports
compatible with the version installed in your project.
Dynamic route parameters
Suppose you define:
<Route path="/items/:id" element={<ItemPage />} />


The :id segment is dynamic.
You can read it using useParams :
import { useParams } from "react-router";


function ItemPage() {
    const { id } = useParams();


    return <h1>Item ID: {id}</h1>;
}


useParams()     returns route parameters as strings, or undefined when absent. Validate them
before treating them as numbers. React Router +1
For a Pokémon detail page, the same concept allows one reusable React component to display
many different Pokémon.

11. A complete practice template
Now let's combine the concepts into a small standalone React application.
This example deliberately uses a small hardcoded dataset instead of implementing your MP2
requirements. It demonstrates reusable components, TypeScript, state, search, sorting, and
CSS.
You can use it to practice the programming concepts before building your actual assignment.
File 1 — App.tsx

import { useState } from "react";
import "./App.css";


interface Item {
     id: number;
     name: string;
     price: number;
}


const initialItems: Item[] = [
     { id: 1, name: "Apple", price: 3 },
     { id: 2, name: "Banana", price: 1 },
     { id: 3, name: "Orange", price: 2 }
];


interface ItemCardProps {
     item: Item;
}


function ItemCard({ item }: ItemCardProps) {
     return (
          <div className="card">
            <h2>{item.name}</h2>
            <p>Price: ${item.price}</p>
          </div>
     );
}

export default function App() {
    const [search, setSearch] = useState("");
    const [ascending, setAscending] = useState(true);


    const visibleItems = initialItems
         .filter(item =>
              item.name.toLowerCase().includes(search.toLowerCase())
         )
         .sort((a, b) =>
              ascending ? a.price - b.price : b.price - a.price
         );


    return (
         <main className="container">
              <h1>My Item Explorer</h1>


              <input
               value={search}
               onChange={e => setSearch(e.target.value)}
               placeholder="Search items..."
              />


              <button onClick={() => setAscending(!ascending)}>
               Sort: {ascending ? "Low to High" : "High to Low"}
              </button>


              <div className="grid">
               {visibleItems.map(item => (
                   <ItemCard key={item.id} item={item} />
               ))}
              </div>
         </main>
    );
}



File 2 — App.css

body {
    margin: 0;
    font-family: Arial, sans-serif;

    background: #f4f5f7;
}


.container {
    max-width: 900px;
    margin: 0 auto;
    padding: 32px;
}


input, button {
    padding: 10px 14px;
    margin: 6px;
    border: 1px solid #ccc;
    border-radius: 8px;
}


button {
    cursor: pointer;
}


.grid {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns:
      repeat(auto-fit, minmax(180px, 1fr));
    gap: 16px;
    margin-top: 24px;
}


.card {
    background: white;
    padding: 18px;
    border-radius: 12px;
    box-shadow: 0 2px 8px #00000012;
}



What the template looks like
Here is an interactive version of the core behavior.

 My Item Explorer
    Search items...                                                                   Low to High

                🍌                               🍊                                 🍎
              Banana                          Orange                             Apple
                $1                              $2                                $3

When you type or click the sort button, React derives a new list from the current state.
Notice that the data is not stored again in a separate visibleItems state variable. It is
calculated from the original data plus the search and sorting state. For a small collection, this is
simple and efficient.
12. Running the practice project in VS Code
First, check whether Node.js and npm are installed:
node -v
npm -v


For a separate practice project, create a Vite React + TypeScript application:
npm create vite@latest react-practice -- --template react-ts


cd react-practice


npm install


npm run dev


Open the local URL printed in your terminal. Then replace the practice project's src/App.tsx
and src/App.css with the examples above.
For your existing MP2 repository, do not recreate the project or overwrite existing files. Use
its existing package configuration and scripts.

Useful commands:
Command                                    Purpose
npm install                                Install project dependencies
npm run dev                                Start local development server
npm run build                              Create production build
npm run preview                            Preview production build
git status                                 See changed files
git add .                                  Stage changes
git commit -m "..."                        Commit changes
git push                                   Push to remote repository
Vite requires a build step before GitHub Pages can serve the production application; for a
repository named mp2 , a project-site deployment normally requires base: "/mp2/" . Vite
13. The most common mistakes
Understanding these will save you considerable debugging time.
Mistake                           Why it's wrong                     Correct idea
onClick={handleClick()}           Calls the function while rendering onClick={handleClick}
array.sort(...)     on state      Mutates the original state array   Sort a copied array
Forgetting key in .map()          React cannot reliably track list   Use a stable unique ID
                                  identity
Using = instead of === in         Assignment instead of strict       Use ===
comparisons                       comparison
Calling hooks inside conditions   Breaks React's hook ordering rules Call hooks at component top
                                                                     level
Accessing data before it loads    May cause runtime errors           Handle null , loading, and
                                                                     errors
Using .map() on a non-array       Runtime error                      Check the API response shape

Ignoring rejected Promises          Errors become unhandled          Use try/catch
React's official guidance is to treat arrays and objects in state as immutable and produce
updated copies instead of changing existing state values. React
14. Test yourself before moving to MP2
Try these questions without referring to the previous examples.
 Fundamentals quiz                                                                    5 questions
 1. What does .filter() return?
     One matching element
     A new array of matching elements
     A sorted array
     A Boolean

 2. What does useState() return?
     A Promise
     A component
     A state value and setter function
     An HTML element

 3. Which syntax reads a dynamic URL parameter?
     useState()
     useParams()
     useEffect()
     axios.get()

 4. What is the purpose of await?

       Stop the entire browser
       Make CSS load
       Wait for a Promise within an async function
       Convert JavaScript to TypeScript

  5. Why use TypeScript interfaces?
      To style HTML
      To define expected object structures
      To start a server
      To make HTTP requests
                                          Check answers

15. What to learn next for your MP2
Now that you've seen the fundamentals, I recommend studying them in this order:
  1. JavaScript array operations — map , filter , sort , find , includes .
  2. TypeScript interfaces and types — modeling Pokémon API responses.
  3. React props and state — making reusable cards and interactive inputs.
  4. API requests and asynchronous programming — retrieving remote data safely.
  5. React Router — creating list, gallery, and detail views.
  6. CSS Flexbox and Grid — creating responsive layouts.
  7. Error handling, testing, and deployment — turning a prototype into a reliable application.
The three ideas I would prioritize above everything else are React state, array transformations,
and async/await. Those are what connect user interaction, API data, and the visual interface.
Once you can explain and modify the practice template without assistance, you'll have the
foundation to implement your own MP2 features rather than memorizing a completed solution.
